From 254da898cdbd987fd76a0364880084ecbcfb4376 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:54:24 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 12/18] WebXR: the screen as an immersive device,
 inline-stereo, and the inline projection

Pages declare stereo only through WebXR: the stereolayout canvas
attribute is removed.

With dom.vr.flat.enabled, the VR service offers the screen itself as an
immersive device (FlatSession: "Stereo display"). An "immersive-vr"
session (WebXR section 3.3) shows its canvas full screen; the session's
frames go back into that canvas through a swap chain of its own, two
views side by side with the left eye first, and the canvas's DMABuf
front buffer is marked so (source XR) and declared to Stereo KWin.

Each eye gets the screen's resolution and an off-axis projection through
the screen as a window, at the viewer's distance
(dom.vr.flat.viewer-distance-mm, 700) with the eyes apart by
dom.vr.flat.eye-separation-mm (64), from the screen's size in
millimetres. Without controllers the mouse turns the view, the wheel and
the arrow keys move it; leaving full screen ends the session.

The canvas keeps its element and size for the page; while it shows the
session's frames, its renderer takes the frames' size, the frames fill
the canvas's box (each eye's view was made for that box) and they go to
the compositor as the canvas's own frames do, as DMABuf. The VR service
takes no texture from the flat display: it only paces the frames.
A session's canvas need not be in the page (the WebXR samples keep
theirs detached): for the session the browser puts it at the end of the
page's root element, full screen, and takes it out again afterwards.
isSessionSupported("immersive-vr") answers at once on the flat display,
the screen being always there: pages may wait only briefly for the
answer before they choose their renderer (StorySplat waits 800 ms, then
takes WebGPU, which has no WebXR binding).

Without the VR process (Linux), the VR service runs in the browser and
joins the same shared memory each time it starts; VRManager clears it
between runtime detection and enumeration, so the browser's states count
again from 1 and the service took the enumeration request for the
detection it had already seen, never finding a device. It now forgets
the last state it saw when it joins again.

Inline sessions take the "inline-stereo" feature (WebXR section 3.4):
the session has a left and a right view through the output canvas as a
window, the canvas holds them side by side (XRWebGLLayer.getViewport),
and its front buffer is marked and declared the same way.

The inline view's projection (section 4.2) is now built as the immersive
views' are, right handed with -Z forward, from the inline vertical field
of view and the canvas's aspect, so a point in front of the viewer is in
front of the camera.

